TI  - LSE-FRGCN: A Logical Schema-Enhanced Fuzzy Relational Graph Convolutional Network for Stance Detection
AU  - Huang, H.
AU  - Dong, L.
AU  - Ma, J.
AU  - Yang, Y.
AU  - Dai, G.
AU  - Zhang, B.
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Consumer Electronics
AB  - Understanding public attitudes expressed on social media platforms forms the foundation of opinion-mining applications, especially within the consumer electronics domain. Stance detection, as a fundamental subtask, aims to identify the viewpoint or position conveyed in subjective texts with regard to specific targets, such as products, features, or brands. Although there have been notable improvements in traditional stance detection methodologies, accurately interpreting texts with inherently ambiguous positions remains a challenging. Fuzzy logic offers a theoretical framework for representing uncertainty in knowledge. However, existing fuzzy-based and LLM-fuzzy approaches typically assume static and homogeneous uncertainty patterns, and their practical application is further hindered by the need for extensive labeled datasets to extract logical rules, which is unrealistic given the diversity of stance detection targets. Drawing on the strong zero-shot reasoning abilities of large language models (LLMs), we introduce a novel architecture termed the LLM-enhanced Fuzzy Relational Graph Convolutional Network with Logical Schema Enhancement (LSE-FRGCN). Unlike prior frameworks that rely on fixed fuzzification schemes, LSE-FRGCN generalizes fuzzy reasoning from static fuzzification to an adaptive and learnable uncertainty modeling paradigm that is conditioned on relational graph structure. This design combines the representational flexibility of large language models with uncertainty-aware relational reasoning for stance detection. Specifically, LSE-FRGCN is structured around two key modules. The first module generates a logical schema graph that abstracts first-order logical rationales into transferable conceptual structures. The second module employs a fuzzy relational graph convolutional network to integrate logical information under adaptive uncertainty modeling. This generalization enables robust stance inference in zero-shot, cross-target, and low-resource scenarios. Experiments show that our method consistently achieves superior results across these settings. In particular, on the challenging zero-shot benchmarks SemEval-2016 and VAST, our approach surpasses the strongest baselines by margins of 5.1% and 5.5%, respectively.  © 2026 IEEE.
