AU  - Pathak, A.
T2  - 6th International Conference on Computer Networks and Inventive Communication Technologies, ICCNCT 2026 - Proceedings
AB  - Deepfake news is rapidly proliferating on social media, endangering digital identity, misinformation management, and public trust. Despite the high accuracy of current deepfake detection techniques in offline settings, their practical application in real world browsing situations is still restricted by computational limitations and a lack of contextual awareness. A context gated, near real time deepfake detection framework for social media analysis is presented in this study. The proposed solution combines a browse- based monitoring pipeline, a FastAPI backend for efficient inference, and a fine tuned EfficientNet B0 image classifier. A zero shot BART MNLI text classifier is used to analyze webpage context to selectively activate detection. The RVF10k dataset, which includes both real images from FFHQ and fake images generated using StyleGAN, is used to train and evaluate the image classifier. The model achieves a validation accuracy of 98 percent with balanced precision and recall across classes. The possibility of implementing deepfake detection models directly within user browsing environments is shown by experimental results, which demonstrate that contextual gating dramatically increases inference efficiency while maintaining high detection performance. © 2026 IEEE.
