TY  - JOUR
TI  - MOTIF: A text-aware and disentangled Mamba-based architecture for multimodal sentiment analysis
AU  - Hu, B.
AU  - Li, Y.
T2  - Knowledge-Based Systems
AB  - Understanding human sentiment is a fundamental goal of affective computing and plays a crucial role in applications such as human-computer interaction, healthcare, and social media analysis. However, multimodal sentiment analysis (MSA) presents enduring challenges due to the heterogeneous, noisy, and temporally complex nature of textual, acoustic, and visual inputs. To address these issues, we propose MOTIF, a lightweight yet expressive framework that integrates disentangled representation learning, hierarchical temporal modeling, and text-guided cross-modal fusion. At its core, MOTIF introduces the BiMamba-X layer, a dual-branch temporal encoder combining bidirectional Mamba with GRU and convolutional refinement to jointly capture long-range dependencies and local emotional dynamics. To reduce redundancy and enhance interpretability, a feature disentanglement module decomposes each modality into shared and modality-specific subspaces, supervised by reconstruction, alignment, consistency, and orthogonality constraints. We further design a Text-aware Modality Enhancement (TAME) mechanism and a Text-guided Query Mamba (TQ-Mamba) module to inject linguistic priors into audio-visual streams and enable fine-grained semantic fusion. In addition, a Text-Guided Asymmetric Temporal Modeling strategy updates only the text pathway during sequence encoding, stabilizing training and anchoring cross-modal interactions. A composite loss function jointly optimizes disentanglement and task-specific prediction. Extensive experiments on the CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI benchmarks show that MOTIF consistently outperforms state-of-the-art models across the majority of classification and regression metrics, while maintaining high parameter efficiency. Ablation studies further confirm the complementary roles of each module, particularly BiMamba-X and the text-guided fusion strategy. We also provide visualization analyses to illustrate the interpretability of the learned representations and cross-modal interactions. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
