TI  - Mitigating Hallucinations in Financial LLMs via Residual-Quantization Retrieval and Weighted Re-ranking
AU  - Wang, G.
AU  - Sun, Y.
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design, CSCWD
A2  - Shen W.
A2  - Barthes J.-P.
A2  - Luo J.
A2  - Shi Y.
A2  - Zhu H.
A2  - Zhang J.
A2  - Peng K.
A2  - Hou L.
AB  - The introduction of information technology has led to a dramatic increase in data volume within the financial industry, and large language models have shown strong capabilities in extracting key information from financial texts. However, these models face challenges in the financial domain, including a lack of Chinese financial text datasets and the tendency to generate hallucinated answers when addressing complex financial knowledge questions. To address these limitations, we construct a financial question-answering dataset based on the open-source financial evaluation dataset OpenFinData, leveraging GLM-4 and the principle of question equivalence. We also integrate heterogeneous data from financial news websites, social media platforms, and company annual reports, which is then cleaned, preprocessed, and consolidated into a comprehensive financial text corpus. To mitigate hallucination in responses to complex and time-sensitive financial knowledge questions, we propose a retrieval-augmented approach combining financial knowledge retrieval with large language models. Experiments show that the retrieval-augmented large language model outperforms in both financial sentiment analysis and financial interpretation tasks. © 2026 IEEE.
