TY  - CONF
TI  - Hybrid CNN-BERT Model with Attention Mechanisms for Classifying Depression, Anxiety, and Stress in Malay Social Media Texts
AU  - Ahmad, Z.
AU  - Conway, M.
AU  - Yusoff, M.
AU  - Maskat, R.
T2  - 2025 6th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Data Sciences: From Insights To Impact: Leveraging AI And Data Science For Strategic Decisions, AiDAS 2025 - Conference Proceedings
AB  - This study presents a hybrid deep learning approach for classifying Depression, Anxiety, and Stress (DAS) in Malay-language social media posts, addressing both the linguistic complexity and data limitations inherent in low-resource languages. The proposed model integrates semantic features from a fine-tuned Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) model with syntactic patterns captured through Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) layers. To enhance interpretability and performance on imbalanced datasets, two attention mechanisms, Bias-Corrected Weighted Attention (BCWA) and Class-Aware Attention Model (CAAM), are incorporated. BCWA corrects class bias by recalibrating feature importance, while CAAM dynamically adjusts attention weights based on class-specific traits. Experimental evaluation on the MyDAS corpus demonstrates significant performance gains, with the hybrid model achieving a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.88, outperforming both standalone CNN and BERT baselines. This approach offers a robust solution for mental health text classification in low-resource contexts, with potential applications in real-time public health monitoring and early intervention systems. © 2025 IEEE.
